tSize` returns the signed notches actually applied (fewer than requested at the min/max clamp bound, 0 on no-change/failure) so the drain worker's loop bookkeeping stays exact at the clamp bound. The earlier v19 approach of posting `WM_MOUSEWHEEL + MK_CONTROL` to the conhost window did not work once a TUI put the console into mouse-input mode (QuickEdit off): conhost forwards both the physical wheel and a posted wheel to the app as input records, so neither reaches conhost's own zoom — that's why zoom worked only before the agent's TUI came up. Setting the font is independent of console input mode, so it works whether the TUI is up or not. WT is left on its native path (dispatch → `HandleMouseHookEvent`, which now only repaints the embedded panel after WT's native wheel zoom). Right-click is no longer intercepted at all (v29.0, root-fix for issue #90): the hook does not touch `WM_RBUTTONDOWN`/`WM_RBUTTONUP` — conhost/Windows handle the right mouse button natively everywhere (context menus in the editor/Solution Explorer, conhost's own QuickEdit-on right-click paste). The earlier "conhost right-click paste fallback" (issues #78/#82/#83) consumed right-clicks via a global `WH_MOUSE_LL` hook to paste into the agent's TUI when QuickEdit was off; despite an occlusion-aware + foreground-gated hit test it kept mis-firing — a right-click elsewhere in VS would be swallowed and the clipboard pushed into the console (issue #90) — so the whole feature was removed (`TryConhostRightClickPaste`, `IsScreenPointOverTerminalWindow`, `_consumeNextRButtonUp`, `_conhostInMouseInputMode` cache + `QueueConhostMouseInputModeRefresh`, and the `WM_RBUTTONDOWN/UP` constants are all gone). Pasting into the agent goes through the prompt box, whose send path delivers via `PasteViaConhostPasteCommandAsync` (conhost's own Edit→Paste `WM_COMMAND/ID_CONSOLE_PASTE` posted straight to `terminalHandle`) regardless of QuickEdit/mouse-input mode — so no terminal right-click paste is needed. `PasteViaConhostPasteCommandAsync` and `IsTerminalInMouseInputMode()` remain, now used only by the send path (the mouse-input-mode probe still drives whether the send path injects its pre-deselect right-click). TUI Fullscreen removed, Claude Code always launches classic (v53.0): the model-menu "TUI Fullscreen" toggle (`TuiFullscreenEnableMenuItem`/`TuiFullscreenDisableMenuItem`, `SendTuiModeCommandAsync`, `ClaudeCodeSettings.ClaudeTuiFullscreen`) is gone — `GetClaudeCommand()` now unconditionally prefixes the launch with `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_ALTERNATE_SCREEN=1` instead of branching on a persisted tri-state setting. This also retired the issue #96 fullscreen-only mouse-wheel-to-PgUp/PgDn forwarding (`IsClaudeFullscreenConhostActive`, `GetFullscreenWheelScrollKeystrokeCount`, `SendScrollKeyToTerminal`, `TryEnsureFullscreenLineScrollKeybinding`/`AddBindingIfAbsent` and the `keybindings.json` line-scroll installer) since fullscreen (alternate-screen) rendering can no longer be entered through the extension, making that whole path permanently unreachable; `ShouldDispatchMouseHookMessage`'s `WM_MOUSEWHEEL` case now dispatches only for Ctrl held, and `HandleMouseHookEvent`'s wheel branch lost its plain-wheel-forward `else if`. Ctrl+Scroll zoom hijacked other apps when VS wasn't foreground (v51.0): both zoom paths — `TryForwardConhostCtrlZoom` (conhost) and the `ctrlDown` branch of `HandleMouseHookEvent` (Windows Terminal) — only hit-tested the cursor against the terminal's screen rectangle, which stays valid even when another maximized window is now layered on top of that same screen region (VS backgrounded). Global `WH_MOUSE_LL` doesn't know z-order, so Ctrl+Scroll in that other app got silently redirected into zooming the (invisible) embedded terminal instead of reaching the app the user was actually looking at. Fixed by extracting `IsTerminalHostForeground()` (the `GetForegroundWindow() == terminalHandle || hostRoot` check, previously inlined at the top of `IsTerminalFocused()`) and requiring it in both zoom paths before the rect hit test runs — cheap enough for the hook thread (same GetForegroundWindow-based short-circuit `IsTerminalFocused()` already relied on). Deliberately stops short of requiring full `IsTerminalFocused()` (keyboard focus on the terminal control specifically) so hover-to-zoom while VS is foreground still works without first clicking into the terminal.

